# Error Handling (Node.js)

Concepts: see `../../patterns/error-handling.mdc`. Below: API and middleware.

## Result Pattern

- **Type:** `Result<T, E> = { success: true; value: T } | { success: false; error: E }`; use for expected failures (validation, not found)
- **Service layer:** Return Result from services; controller checks and maps to HTTP or throws
- **Errors:** Use AppError with code, message, statusCode; optional details for validation errors

## API Errors

- **RFC 7807 ProblemDetails:** type, title, status, detail, instance, extensions; set Content-Type application/problem+json
- **Status:** Map domain errors to 400, 404, 409, etc.; 500 for unexpected; do not leak stack in production
- **Middleware:** Global error handler catches thrown errors and async rejections; map to status and body; log with correlation ID

## Async

- Use async/await; do not leave promises unhandled; forward errors to error middleware (next(err) or throw)
- In Express: wrap async route handlers so rejections are passed to next

## Conventions

- Result for expected failures; throw for programming errors; single global error handler; consistent response shape; log errors with context

## Anti-Patterns

- Swallowing errors; returning 200 on error; no global handler; exposing stack traces in prod

